{
  "id": "UP01Z",
  "version": "2.0",
  "title": "Bladder neck resection",
  "description": "A bladder neck resection corrects bladder outlet obstruction or stricture that blocks urine flow. During this outpatient or brief inpatient procedure, your urologist uses an endoscope (camera) passed through the urethra to reach the bladder neck and either incise scar tissue, resect obstructive tissue, or perform sphincterotomy (cutting the muscle). The procedure takes 20-60 minutes and is less invasive than open surgery. You may have a temporary catheter for 24-48 hours. Most people experience improved urinary flow within weeks. Recovery typically takes 2-4 weeks. Follow-up ensures your urine flow remains good. This type of procedure can be performed various ways, including incision / contracture release, resection, and sphincterotomy, and the cost may vary depending on the specific approach or service provided.",
